WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.200
OK.

00:00:01.879 --> 00:00:03.640
Today I turned 40.

00:00:04.139 --> 00:00:04.373
Yeah,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.759
the day I'm recording this video is my lunar birthday.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.813
And on my 40th birthday,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:10.452
I made a decision.

00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:12.980
I'm going to stop hustling so hard.

00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:15.932
Now, I know that might sound like I've made enough money,

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:16.713
and I'm ready to lie flat.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:17.780
Actually, it's the exact opposite.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.432
It's one of the hardest things I've ever done

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:20.612
in my life.

00:00:20.679 --> 00:00:23.073
Because for the past 40 years,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.719
I realized the only thing I know how to do is be tense and push hard.

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:27.172
But now I realize,

00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:29.373
if I want to unlock the next chapter of my life,

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.219
I need something completely new, and that's relaxation.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:34.372
What?

00:00:34.439 --> 00:00:35.213
Relaxation? That's it?

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.052
I know what you're thinking, but hear me out.

00:00:37.119 --> 00:00:38.859
Most people get relaxation completely wrong.

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:40.832
Relaxing isn't lying flat, it's not just having fun,

00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:43.993
and it's definitely not a reward you only earn after you succeed.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.732
Instead, it's the prerequisite for breaking through your bottlenecks

00:00:45.799 --> 00:00:47.280
and opening the next door.

00:00:47.939 --> 00:00:49.633
And the reason I'm making it my life's focus at 40

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.299
comes down to three things: a point of confusion,

00:00:52.409 --> 00:00:54.439
a scar on my wrist, and a book.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.619
What's your name?

00:00:56.719 --> 00:00:57.920
Ray.

00:00:58.259 --> 00:00:59.340
Ray, I'm Ray.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:00.679
Thank you, thanks.

00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:03.420
So that confusion came from a quote by Naval.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.172
He said, if you want to get rich,

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:08.553
spend your time learning, earning, or relaxing,

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.599
outsource or ignore everything else.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.153
Learning and earning, I get that.

00:01:12.219 --> 00:01:14.793
I've been doing those two things for the last decade,

00:01:14.859 --> 00:01:17.280
and it's what I always preach on this channel and in my community.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.733
But why spend time relaxing?

00:01:19.799 --> 00:01:22.260
I could never really wrap my head around it systematically.

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:23.093
Then, earlier this year,

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:25.443
I randomly read a really old book called

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.413
Psycho-Cybernetics,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:28.733
translated as Xin Li Kong Zhi Shu.

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:29.513
After reading it,

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:32.373
it cleared up my confusion about Naval's philosophy on relaxing.

00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:35.433
So today, I want to use my own experiences to talk about

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.093
why, if we want to unlock life's next chapter,

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:40.213
every one of us needs to learn how to relax.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.259
I'll just have a hot mocha, easy to sip.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.713
You guys always see me wearing this

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.093
Apple Watch in my videos. Honestly, I don't really like wearing watches.

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:50.093
I wear it mostly to cover up a massive

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:50.959
scar on my left wrist.

00:01:51.879 --> 00:01:52.313
Hey, don't overthink it,

00:01:52.379 --> 00:01:53.900
I didn't cut myself or anything.

00:01:54.319 --> 00:01:55.633
But this scar,

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.633
in a way, is a microcosm of my tense, wound-up life.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.739
This is my birthday dinner.

00:01:59.959 --> 00:02:02.333
When I was a freshman in high school, my mom opened a small restaurant

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.093
outside the coal mine where we lived.

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:05.159
It was a tiny business, but grueling work.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.593
Whenever I went home for the weekend, I'd go help out,

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:09.212
often working late into the night,

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.139
and then we'd push our tricycle back home.

00:02:11.419 --> 00:02:13.393
One day, I think the gas tank ran out or something,

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:14.639
I forget the exact details.

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:16.832
I just remember it was just me and my mom,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.692
and a bunch of customers were waiting, getting super impatient.

00:02:18.759 --> 00:02:22.272
So I took a kitchen knife to chop some wood and help start a fire.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.652
But I accidentally hacked right into my left wrist.

00:02:24.719 --> 00:02:26.192
Blood just started spraying everywhere.

00:02:26.259 --> 00:02:28.272
I could literally see the bone. I was terrified.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.580
But I didn't dare tell my mom, afraid I'd interrupt her work.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.733
I just told her I cut my hand by accident,

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.599
grabbed my wrist, and ran to a small clinic.

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:36.132
The doctor there stopped the bleeding

00:02:36.199 --> 00:02:38.132
and gave me a bunch of stitches. The clinic was super sketchy,

00:02:38.199 --> 00:02:39.599
and the doctor's skills were pretty limited.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.120
So when the wound healed, the nerves were messed up.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.312
To this day, the area around my thumb is totally numb.

00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:46.432
I can't feel a thing there, but if you touch the scar,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.259
it hurts like crazy and gets super tingly.

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:50.599
So if anyone ever gets into a fight with me,

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:52.192
all they have to do is hit my left wrist hard,

00:02:52.259 --> 00:02:53.120
and they can take me down instantly.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.252
When I got back to school with this injury and people asked

00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:56.612
what happened,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:57.879
I started lying.

00:02:57.979 --> 00:03:00.733
I said I was playing around with a kitchen knife

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.539
and accidentally slashed myself while hacking at stuff.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.712
And this lie had a few different versions.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:05.939
Like sometimes,

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:08.652
depending on who I was talking to, I'd say I got slashed

00:03:08.719 --> 00:03:09.712
in a fight when I was a kid.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.219
It made me sound tough.

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:13.072
I told these lies so many times

00:03:13.139 --> 00:03:14.832
that eventually, I kind of believed them myself.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.000
It became my default answer whenever anyone asked.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.400
I didn't even realize I was lying anymore.

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:23.212
So why did I lie?

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.812
Originally, it was just because I didn't want people looking down on me.

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:27.233
Other kids went on vacations during their time off,

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.972
while I spent my holidays working for survival,

00:03:30.039 --> 00:03:32.219
helping out in a tiny, run-down restaurant,

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.599
and hacking myself in a moment of panic.

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:34.552
Later on,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.932
I just wanted to hide the scar and everything it represented

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:37.733
deep down inside,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.013
to give myself a goal and the strength

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:40.439
to change my destiny against all odds.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.612
So I just kept holding my breath, pushing through,

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.599
trying to force my fate to change through sheer hustle.

00:03:45.139 --> 00:03:46.592
My sophomore year of high school, I worked insanely hard.

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:49.572
I studied until 1 or 2 AM every night and woke up at 5.

00:03:49.639 --> 00:03:51.812
I shot up to third place in my science track,

00:03:51.879 --> 00:03:53.793
and kept my grades top-tier all the way through senior year.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:55.513
Everyone thought I was going to a top university.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.479
But I bombed the entrance exam and only got into a second-tier college.

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:02.072
But then a magical thing happened when I went to this

00:04:02.139 --> 00:04:02.432
second-tier college.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.372
Without the pressure of the gaokao, without everyone's expectations and eyes on me,

00:04:05.439 --> 00:04:07.240
I actually started to let loose and be myself.

00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:11.099
I started reading, listening to English podcasts, even working part-time

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:11.893
and starting mini-businesses.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.893
That period was the fastest time of growth and transformation

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:15.412
in my entire life.

00:04:15.479 --> 00:04:17.533
In a way, it made me who I am today.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.973
OK, OK, I gotta stop here, or I could talk about myself

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:20.840
all day.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.552
So why am I telling you all this?

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.772
What does it have to do with relaxing?

00:04:24.839 --> 00:04:27.272
Well, after reading Psycho-Cybernetics,

00:04:27.339 --> 00:04:30.272
and looking back at this and many similar experiences in my life,

00:04:30.339 --> 00:04:31.413
I noticed a pattern with myself.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.480
Whenever I'm tense and wound up, I usually mess things up.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:35.932
To use a trendy phrase,

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.952
the harder I try, the worse I fail.

00:04:38.019 --> 00:04:41.139
And it's only when I relax that good things actually happen.

00:04:41.399 --> 00:04:42.452
I've been saying this phrase a lot lately.

00:04:42.519 --> 00:04:45.012
It's "Good things only happen when

00:04:45.079 --> 00:04:45.879
you are relaxed."

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.173
Why is that?

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.473
In the book, the author talks about a success mechanism.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.240
In English, it's called the success mechanism.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:55.832
He says that built into every one of our subconscious minds

00:04:55.899 --> 00:04:56.432
is an

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.853
auto-navigation system, just like a missile's guidance system.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.339
It automatically corrects your course and guides you

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:03.192
toward your target.

00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:05.173
You don't need to tell it how to take every single step.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.500
You just set the target and let it run.

00:05:09.199 --> 00:05:11.180
It's basically what we mean when we say "manifesting your desires."

00:05:11.399 --> 00:05:14.252
There's a quote in The Alchemist that goes,

00:05:14.319 --> 00:05:16.473
"When you want something,

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:18.439
all the universe conspires in helping you to achieve it."

00:05:18.899 --> 00:05:21.480
I'm sure most people have had experiences like this.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.312
But here's the catch: this system has one condition to operate.

00:05:25.379 --> 00:05:27.399
You have to be relaxed enough.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.812
When you're tense, anxious,

00:05:29.879 --> 00:05:32.072
and desperately trying to micromanage every single step,

00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:34.512
your conscious mind forces its way in

00:05:34.579 --> 00:05:37.350
and tries to hijack the auto-pilot. And the result is,

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:38.460
you just jam the whole system up.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.812
It's like walking while carrying a full glass of water,

00:05:40.879 --> 00:05:43.673
trying not to spill it. The more you think, "Don't spill,"

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.199
and the harder you stare at the glass, the more you end up spilling.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.752
But if you ignore it, stop caring, and just relax,

00:05:48.819 --> 00:05:50.060
the water stays right in the cup.

00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:51.793
The book refers to this

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.660
as the creative mechanism.

00:05:53.819 --> 00:05:55.533
It gets locked down by your forebrain.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:58.079
So the more you want it, the worse you perform.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:02.372
Specifically, your inability to relax holds you back

00:06:02.439 --> 00:06:03.240
in these four dimensions.

00:06:05.555 --> 00:06:07.495
We often think that pressure comes from motivation.

00:06:07.696 --> 00:06:09.949
I pushed myself to study hard to crush the gaokao,

00:06:10.016 --> 00:06:11.716
driven by this core belief.

00:06:12.216 --> 00:06:14.795
Counterintuitively though, that understanding might be completely wrong.

00:06:15.396 --> 00:06:16.168
The book mentions an experiment:

00:06:16.235 --> 00:06:18.728
when animals learn about a new environment

00:06:18.795 --> 00:06:19.428
without too much pressure,

00:06:19.495 --> 00:06:22.528
they form a very broad cognitive map,

00:06:22.596 --> 00:06:23.988
exploring all sorts of possibilities

00:06:24.055 --> 00:06:26.375
and gaining a comprehensive understanding of their surroundings.

00:06:26.755 --> 00:06:28.088
But if the pressure is super high,

00:06:28.156 --> 00:06:30.348
that cognitive map shrinks drastically.

00:06:30.415 --> 00:06:32.408
They only focus on the most urgent thing right in front of them,

00:06:32.475 --> 00:06:33.548
and go completely blind to everything else.

00:06:33.615 --> 00:06:36.269
"Pressure retards learning."

00:06:36.336 --> 00:06:38.608
Pressure directly slows down your learning.

00:06:38.675 --> 00:06:42.329
And it doesn't just make you slow; it makes your learning narrow. The higher the pressure,

00:06:42.396 --> 00:06:43.949
the more inefficient and narrow your learning becomes.

00:06:44.016 --> 00:06:45.656
This makes you inflexible.

00:06:46.096 --> 00:06:48.248
When you're finally facing a real challenge,

00:06:48.315 --> 00:06:49.475
your performance naturally drops.

00:06:50.036 --> 00:06:51.848
That explains why I messed up my college entrance exam.

00:06:51.915 --> 00:06:55.069
Because I didn't just fail from being too nervous

00:06:55.136 --> 00:06:56.748
and jamming my success mechanism.

00:06:56.815 --> 00:06:59.548
Fundamentally, learning under all that pressure made my scope

00:06:59.615 --> 00:07:00.569
way too narrow.

00:07:00.636 --> 00:07:02.829
I only knew how to take tests, and only specific kinds of tests.

00:07:02.896 --> 00:07:04.276
My whole way of thinking was completely rigid.

00:07:04.675 --> 00:07:06.468
So when faced with a real challenge like the Gaokao,

00:07:06.536 --> 00:07:08.435
I had zero flexibility and no ability to adapt.

00:07:08.696 --> 00:07:10.248
Looking at the bigger picture,

00:07:10.315 --> 00:07:12.769
this also explains a phenomenon a lot of people feel but can't quite

00:07:12.836 --> 00:07:13.149
put into words.

00:07:13.216 --> 00:07:15.868
Why is it that "small-town test machines" like me,

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:17.209
who didn't come from a great background,

00:07:17.276 --> 00:07:19.968
did well in school and were great at taking tests,

00:07:20.036 --> 00:07:23.368
but once we enter society, we end up performing worse than kids

00:07:23.435 --> 00:07:24.209
who grew up in more relaxed environments,

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:26.235
even if their grades weren't as good?

00:07:26.735 --> 00:07:29.248
We used to think it was just because they had more resources

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:30.048
or better connections.

00:07:30.115 --> 00:07:32.468
But this book made me realize there's a deeper reason:

00:07:32.536 --> 00:07:34.848
we thought we were learning better,

00:07:34.915 --> 00:07:36.555
but actually, we were learning much narrower.

00:07:36.755 --> 00:07:39.149
They grew up in a more relaxed environment,

00:07:39.216 --> 00:07:41.048
so their cognitive map is way broader than ours.

00:07:41.115 --> 00:07:44.608
Meanwhile, those small-town test machines are much more likely

00:07:44.675 --> 00:07:45.475
to face high-pressure situations.

00:07:45.815 --> 00:07:48.168
So at the end of the day, "happy learning" actually makes sense.

00:07:48.235 --> 00:07:51.368
And our high-pressure background has been affecting our entire life

00:07:51.435 --> 00:07:51.728
trajectory.

00:07:51.795 --> 00:07:54.168
Now, this isn't to say that studying and working hard under massive pressure

00:07:54.235 --> 00:07:55.829
can't bring you success.

00:07:55.896 --> 00:07:58.209
But the secret is, if you want to unlock even greater success,

00:07:58.276 --> 00:08:02.036
you need to give yourself room to breathe and learn how to relax.

00:08:02.115 --> 00:08:03.848
I know what you're gonna say: "Easy for you to say!

00:08:03.915 --> 00:08:05.935
You can't just relax on command."

00:08:06.115 --> 00:08:07.908
"What if my circumstances don't allow me to relax?"

00:08:07.975 --> 00:08:10.748
"Rui, when you were a small-town test machine, you couldn't

00:08:10.815 --> 00:08:11.668
relax either, right?"

00:08:11.735 --> 00:08:13.009
"Now that you're well-off,

00:08:13.076 --> 00:08:14.216
it's easy for you to preach."

00:08:14.975 --> 00:08:17.588
Yeah, I don't deny that. For a lot of people in crisis mode,

00:08:17.656 --> 00:08:18.975
it's impossible to completely let go.

00:08:19.076 --> 00:08:20.709
But we have to understand that "relaxing"

00:08:20.776 --> 00:08:23.836
is actually a spectrum, not just a single state.

00:08:24.336 --> 00:08:26.129
And that brings me to my second point:

00:08:26.196 --> 00:08:30.308
your inability to relax is an overreaction.

00:08:30.375 --> 00:08:33.729
It turns you into a reactor in life, rather than an

00:08:33.796 --> 00:08:34.596
actor.

00:08:36.395 --> 00:08:39.269
The book says humans are fundamentally actors, not reactors.

00:08:39.336 --> 00:08:41.908
We aren't just ships drifting in the wind. We have goals

00:08:41.975 --> 00:08:42.775
and we have direction.

00:08:42.855 --> 00:08:45.168
If your reaction to your environment isn't pushing your goals forward,

00:08:45.235 --> 00:08:47.229
or is even holding you back,

00:08:47.296 --> 00:08:49.456
what you really should do is just not react.

00:08:49.995 --> 00:08:52.975
The worrying, the anxiety,

00:08:53.096 --> 00:08:54.968
and the stressing we do every day—that's exactly this kind of useless reaction.

00:08:55.035 --> 00:08:57.149
They don't help you move forward; they only keep you stuck.

00:08:57.216 --> 00:08:59.076
What you really need to do is stop reacting.

00:08:59.275 --> 00:08:59.849
For example, the book points out that

00:08:59.916 --> 00:09:02.489
the reason most of us worry, get anxious, and panic

00:09:02.556 --> 00:09:04.649
essentially stems from a really terrible mental habit:

00:09:04.716 --> 00:09:07.395
you always feel like you should be doing a million things right now.

00:09:07.796 --> 00:09:08.729
While you're working on Thing A,

00:09:08.796 --> 00:09:11.068
you suddenly remember Thing B isn't done, you want to do Thing C,

00:09:11.135 --> 00:09:11.975
and Thing D can't be delayed either.

00:09:12.395 --> 00:09:13.989
And the next passage from the author

00:09:14.056 --> 00:09:15.068
is something I absolutely love.

00:09:15.135 --> 00:09:17.968
He says that even on your busiest day,

00:09:18.035 --> 00:09:21.288
every single task can only come to you one by one.

00:09:21.355 --> 00:09:21.808
It has to.

00:09:21.875 --> 00:09:25.009
No matter how many problems, tasks, or pressures you have,

00:09:25.076 --> 00:09:28.255
they can only line up single file and come at you one at a time.

00:09:28.655 --> 00:09:29.495
That's just an objective law of reality.

00:09:30.056 --> 00:09:32.869
So when you have a million things swirling in your head,

00:09:32.936 --> 00:09:34.895
you're actually violating that reality.

00:09:35.035 --> 00:09:38.296
Because we overreact, we end up procrastinating, avoiding things,

00:09:38.436 --> 00:09:40.936
overthinking, worrying, or even getting crushed by the pressure.

00:09:41.535 --> 00:09:44.948
We fail to act as the one in control, deciding what to focus on

00:09:45.015 --> 00:09:46.168
in this exact moment,

00:09:46.235 --> 00:09:47.395
and that leads to us doing a terrible job at everything.

00:09:47.895 --> 00:09:50.989
That's why so many productivity books emphasize single-tasking

00:09:51.056 --> 00:09:51.408
and push back against

00:09:51.475 --> 00:09:52.708
multitasking.

00:09:52.775 --> 00:09:55.788
And when books teach us mindfulness and focusing on the present,

00:09:55.855 --> 00:09:59.408
they're essentially just following this exact same rule of taking things

00:09:59.475 --> 00:10:00.428
one step at a time.

00:10:00.495 --> 00:10:02.216
They're telling us to relax and stop overreacting.

00:10:02.596 --> 00:10:05.349
It's just that they usually focus more on the spiritual or wellness side

00:10:05.416 --> 00:10:06.535
without really explaining the underlying mechanics.

00:10:06.916 --> 00:10:09.149
But this book figured it all out and explained the logic perfectly

00:10:09.216 --> 00:10:10.196
over half a century ago.

00:10:10.995 --> 00:10:12.908
And this applies not just to a specific task,

00:10:12.975 --> 00:10:15.355
but to our entire life journey.

00:10:15.816 --> 00:10:19.208
We always say "life is a journey," but honestly,

00:10:19.275 --> 00:10:22.288
a lot of times we don't really grasp what that

00:10:22.355 --> 00:10:22.908
actually means.

00:10:22.975 --> 00:10:25.788
In recent years, a lot of books have been preaching "enjoy the process"

00:10:25.855 --> 00:10:26.655
and downplaying the importance of goals.

00:10:26.855 --> 00:10:28.448
Early this year on our channel, we read a book

00:10:28.515 --> 00:10:30.408
called Tiny Experiments,

00:10:30.475 --> 00:10:32.908
which even goes as far as saying you shouldn't set goals at all,

00:10:32.975 --> 00:10:34.155
you should set experiments.

00:10:34.355 --> 00:10:36.129
But that totally contradicts another popular narrative.

00:10:36.196 --> 00:10:38.155
There are endless "success" books

00:10:38.336 --> 00:10:41.255
and productivity guides teaching you how to set goals, make plans,

00:10:41.395 --> 00:10:42.028
and break down execution.

00:10:42.096 --> 00:10:43.968
So which one is actually right?

00:10:44.035 --> 00:10:46.208
Well, Psycho-Cybernetics gives a crystal clear answer:

00:10:46.275 --> 00:10:49.135
they're both right, you've just messed up the ratio.

00:10:51.155 --> 00:10:54.229
The book says humans are naturally goal-oriented animals.

00:10:54.296 --> 00:10:56.275
Without a goal, you wouldn't even start.

00:10:56.416 --> 00:10:58.048
So how could there even be a process?

00:10:58.115 --> 00:10:59.385
Therefore, goals are absolutely necessary.

00:10:59.755 --> 00:11:01.249
But once you've set that goal,

00:11:01.316 --> 00:11:04.115
you should actually be in "journey mode" for the vast majority of the time.

00:11:04.635 --> 00:11:06.489
In journey mode, you are an actor.

00:11:06.556 --> 00:11:08.149
You decide exactly where to step,

00:11:08.216 --> 00:11:10.115
and you even get to decide to enjoy the scenery along the way.

00:11:10.535 --> 00:11:12.688
But when you're only staring at the goal, you end up overreacting.

00:11:12.755 --> 00:11:14.548
Trying too hard turns you into a reactor,

00:11:14.615 --> 00:11:15.716
and you end up losing your footing.

00:11:16.196 --> 00:11:18.408
What you really should do is spend most of your time watching the road

00:11:18.475 --> 00:11:18.788
right under your feet,

00:11:18.855 --> 00:11:20.635
and only occasionally look up to make sure you're still heading the right way.

00:11:21.015 --> 00:11:23.349
Yet so many of us do the exact opposite.

00:11:23.416 --> 00:11:27.448
We spend too little time in journey mode and stare way too hard at the goal. The more you stare,

00:11:27.515 --> 00:11:30.129
the more the pressure pushes you from being an actor to a reactor,

00:11:30.196 --> 00:11:31.535
and you end up moving slower by trying to rush.

00:11:31.755 --> 00:11:34.735
The heavier your goals are, the more you need to learn how to relax.

00:11:34.895 --> 00:11:36.576
This is incredibly relevant to my life right now.

00:11:38.635 --> 00:11:41.269
I've mentioned before that I've achieved relative financial and time

00:11:41.336 --> 00:11:41.629
freedom,

00:11:41.696 --> 00:11:44.135
and now I only work on things I love, aiming for big goals.

00:11:44.696 --> 00:11:46.948
But I found that with such massive goals and so many projects,

00:11:47.015 --> 00:11:50.109
I'm constantly trying to solve every single problem in front of me

00:11:50.176 --> 00:11:50.509
as quickly as possible.

00:11:50.576 --> 00:11:51.869
It leaves me feeling suffocated.

00:11:51.936 --> 00:11:54.168
Sometimes I get so overwhelmed by the tasks—

00:11:54.235 --> 00:11:56.408
wanting to do everything right now but ending up doing nothing at all—

00:11:56.475 --> 00:11:58.926
that I forget to focus on the process and even want to just give up.

00:11:59.696 --> 00:12:01.629
And worse, it makes me beat myself up,

00:12:01.696 --> 00:12:03.048
thinking I'm just not capable enough.

00:12:03.115 --> 00:12:06.448
And that brings us to the next major way pressure ruins our

00:12:06.515 --> 00:12:06.869
progress.

00:12:06.936 --> 00:12:09.749
My 40th birthday is over. Let's go to the studio.

00:12:09.816 --> 00:12:12.596
Let's see if that security cat is still there.

00:12:17.926 --> 00:12:19.999
The other day on Twitter, I saw a highly debated clip

00:12:20.066 --> 00:12:21.765
of the billionaire Marc Andreessen.

00:12:21.966 --> 00:12:22.946
It sparked a ton of controversy.

00:12:23.306 --> 00:12:26.658
In an interview, he said he rarely self-reflects.

00:12:26.725 --> 00:12:29.739
He's even read over 400 biographies of great entrepreneurs,

00:12:29.806 --> 00:12:32.046
and found that almost none of them engage in self-reflection.

00:12:32.186 --> 00:12:33.198
"You don't have any levels of

00:12:33.265 --> 00:12:33.999
introspection."

00:12:34.066 --> 00:12:35.826
"Yes, zero. As little as possible." "Why?"

00:12:36.605 --> 00:12:39.558
"Move forward. Go. Yeah, I, I don't know."

00:12:39.625 --> 00:12:40.716
"I just find people who dwell on the

00:12:40.785 --> 00:12:42.139
past get stuck in the past. It's, it's

00:12:42.206 --> 00:12:43.216
just, it's a real problem and it's a,

00:12:43.365 --> 00:12:44.218
it's a problem at work and it's a

00:12:44.285 --> 00:12:47.318
problem at home." He criticized self-reflection as a modern

00:12:47.385 --> 00:12:48.778
invention,

00:12:48.846 --> 00:12:51.838
a psychological and philosophical movement driven by guilt.

00:12:51.905 --> 00:12:53.418
Great figures of the past didn't do this.

00:12:53.485 --> 00:12:56.318
They only looked outward and forward to build great things,

00:12:56.385 --> 00:12:58.158
instead of sitting around criticizing themselves

00:12:58.225 --> 00:12:59.586
and doubting themselves while living in the past.

00:12:59.686 --> 00:13:01.958
As soon as he said that, the internet blew up.

00:13:02.025 --> 00:13:04.338
His critics and his supporters started arguing fiercely,

00:13:04.405 --> 00:13:06.139
and Marc himself didn't let it go either—

00:13:06.206 --> 00:13:09.038
he kept getting into endless debates with his haters

00:13:09.105 --> 00:13:09.318
right there on Twitter.

00:13:09.385 --> 00:13:12.278
Now, I know his statement is definitely extreme,

00:13:12.346 --> 00:13:13.838
but after I watched it, I thought,

00:13:13.905 --> 00:13:17.099
doesn't this perfectly align with the point I've been trying to

00:13:17.166 --> 00:13:17.538
make?

00:13:17.605 --> 00:13:20.918
If we apply this idea of "self-reflection" to the success mechanism

00:13:20.985 --> 00:13:21.999
we've been talking about today,

00:13:22.066 --> 00:13:23.426
it's basically an error-correction mechanism.

00:13:24.005 --> 00:13:26.458
There's nothing inherently wrong with correcting errors. Your success mechanism needs it,

00:13:26.525 --> 00:13:29.239
just like a missile has to constantly adjust its trajectory to hit the

00:13:29.306 --> 00:13:29.519
target.

00:13:29.586 --> 00:13:32.198
But the book immediately follows up with a crucial prerequisite:

00:13:32.265 --> 00:13:34.338
for this error correction to actually work,

00:13:34.405 --> 00:13:37.105
it has to be subconscious, automatic and

00:13:37.245 --> 00:13:38.125
spontaneous.

00:13:38.285 --> 00:13:41.359
Meaning, the correction has to happen at a subconscious level.

00:13:41.426 --> 00:13:42.798
It has to be automatic and unprompted.

00:13:42.865 --> 00:13:46.019
Any conscious effort to engage in self-criticism and self-

00:13:46.086 --> 00:13:46.599
correction

00:13:46.666 --> 00:13:49.846
is fundamentally over-correcting, and it does more harm than good.

00:13:50.546 --> 00:13:51.099
Why?

00:13:51.166 --> 00:13:53.359
For example, if you're giving a speech on stage,

00:13:53.426 --> 00:13:56.379
your eyes will naturally scan the audience's faces.

00:13:56.446 --> 00:13:57.458
And if someone frowns,

00:13:57.525 --> 00:13:59.259
you might subconsciously slow down your pace,

00:13:59.326 --> 00:14:00.846
or explain things in a different way.

00:14:01.306 --> 00:14:03.879
You won't even realize you're doing it.

00:14:03.946 --> 00:14:05.625
That's course correction at the subconscious level.

00:14:06.206 --> 00:14:08.418
It runs quietly in the background, highly accurate,

00:14:08.485 --> 00:14:09.285
and very efficient.

00:14:09.666 --> 00:14:11.639
But if, during your speech,

00:14:11.706 --> 00:14:13.879
someone suddenly stands up and boos you, or

00:14:13.946 --> 00:14:16.078
the voice of self-criticism in your head gets loud enough,

00:14:16.145 --> 00:14:17.259
then this correction signal

00:14:17.326 --> 00:14:20.338
gets dragged from the subconscious right into your conscious mind.

00:14:20.405 --> 00:14:21.938
Your focus is completely shattered.

00:14:22.005 --> 00:14:25.158
You start doubting and second-guessing yourself, your system overloads,

00:14:25.225 --> 00:14:26.046
and you panic.

00:14:26.645 --> 00:14:29.479
The exact same signal helps you when it's subconscious,

00:14:29.546 --> 00:14:31.745
but becomes pure interference when it hits the conscious level.

00:14:32.385 --> 00:14:33.666
That's exactly how self-criticism works.

00:14:34.245 --> 00:14:35.859
Of course you need to correct mistakes and improve,

00:14:35.926 --> 00:14:38.838
but you should let your subconscious handle that process—

00:14:38.905 --> 00:14:40.385
auto-correcting and auto-adjusting.

00:14:40.765 --> 00:14:42.538
When you drag it into your conscious mind,

00:14:42.605 --> 00:14:45.078
turning it into "Why am I so bad?" or "I messed up again,"

00:14:45.145 --> 00:14:48.318
or "Am I just a failure?"—then you're no longer correcting,

00:14:48.385 --> 00:14:49.686
you're attacking yourself.

00:14:50.346 --> 00:14:52.838
Your success mechanism isn't being fixed,

00:14:52.905 --> 00:14:54.166
it's being suppressed.

00:14:54.446 --> 00:14:56.619
So when we talk about relaxing,

00:14:56.686 --> 00:14:59.038
it really just means letting your conscious mind step back

00:14:59.105 --> 00:15:00.105
and letting your subconscious take the wheel.

00:15:00.586 --> 00:15:03.145
Being tense is just your conscious mind fighting for control.

00:15:03.645 --> 00:15:05.178
When your success mechanism is suppressed,

00:15:05.245 --> 00:15:07.946
the most obvious symptom is that you're afraid to be yourself.

00:15:08.125 --> 00:15:09.399
Why emphasize "being yourself"?

00:15:09.466 --> 00:15:11.658
Because deep down, every single one of us has a true

00:15:11.725 --> 00:15:11.899
self,

00:15:11.966 --> 00:15:13.765
and a natural instinct to express it.

00:15:14.306 --> 00:15:17.259
True success is finding and unleashing that authentic

00:15:17.326 --> 00:15:17.979
version of you,

00:15:18.046 --> 00:15:20.578
and answering that ultimate question: "Who am I?"

00:15:20.645 --> 00:15:23.666
But excessive self-criticism actively crushes that expression.

00:15:24.446 --> 00:15:25.639
I often say that as a YouTuber,

00:15:25.706 --> 00:15:27.119
succeeding is actually really simple:

00:15:27.186 --> 00:15:30.666
you just need to take how you express yourself off-camera

00:15:30.905 --> 00:15:32.145
and bring that exact same energy on-camera.

00:15:32.285 --> 00:15:34.666
Off-camera, when nobody's around, you can

00:15:34.785 --> 00:15:37.519
make goofy faces in the mirror, sing freely, crack jokes,

00:15:37.586 --> 00:15:38.385
totally full of confidence.

00:15:38.505 --> 00:15:41.245
But the second the camera rolls, you become a totally different person.

00:15:41.645 --> 00:15:44.926
You can tangibly feel yourself holding back.

00:15:45.385 --> 00:15:48.338
But notice that most of the time, what's suppressing you isn't the

00:15:48.405 --> 00:15:49.278
audience,

00:15:49.346 --> 00:15:51.666
it's you. It's your own conscious mind.

00:15:52.326 --> 00:15:55.558
When you drop that self-consciousness and hit a state of flow,

00:15:55.625 --> 00:15:58.058
that's when you achieve your maximum self-expression,

00:15:58.125 --> 00:15:59.706
and that is your ultimate potential.

00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:02.885
And this state of flow is the deepest form of relaxation.

00:16:03.586 --> 00:16:05.566
Very few people can actually pull it off, myself included.

00:16:06.086 --> 00:16:08.505
But for those who can, it's almost impossible for them not to succeed.

00:16:08.706 --> 00:16:12.739
Now, the stage or camera example is specific, but it's also

00:16:12.806 --> 00:16:13.698
universal,

00:16:13.765 --> 00:16:16.619
because we're all performing on the stage of our own lives.

00:16:16.686 --> 00:16:19.178
A lot of the time, we don't even realize we're suppressing

00:16:19.245 --> 00:16:19.778
ourselves.

00:16:19.846 --> 00:16:21.338
And the more you suppress yourself,

00:16:21.405 --> 00:16:23.979
refusing to release your unique personality and nature,

00:16:24.046 --> 00:16:26.546
the further away you get from the success you want.

00:16:27.346 --> 00:16:28.918
That's why, at 40 years old,

00:16:28.985 --> 00:16:31.058
I decided to pursue just being "loose,"

00:16:31.125 --> 00:16:32.865
to capture that feeling of true relaxation.

00:16:33.505 --> 00:16:34.479
I get the logic,

00:16:34.546 --> 00:16:36.879
but so often I still can't help it.

00:16:36.946 --> 00:16:39.645
I still tense up and consciously criticize myself.

00:16:40.125 --> 00:16:42.798
So that leads to what I want to share next—the most important lesson I learned from

00:16:42.865 --> 00:16:46.019
Psycho-Cybernetics:

00:16:46.086 --> 00:16:48.546
and that is saying goodbye to the straw man from your past.

00:16:51.806 --> 00:16:53.778
Going back to that Marc Andreessen interview, did you notice?

00:16:53.846 --> 00:16:57.818
He said self-criticism is a guilt-driven psychological

00:16:57.885 --> 00:16:58.058
philosophy,

00:16:58.125 --> 00:17:00.505
and people trapped in it usually live in the past.

00:17:00.985 --> 00:17:02.958
Well, this book mentions the exact same thing.

00:17:03.025 --> 00:17:05.638
The author says, "Don't fight the straw man out

00:17:05.705 --> 00:17:08.786
of the past." Stop wrestling with the ghost of yesterday.

00:17:09.326 --> 00:17:12.618
A lot of the tension and self-criticism you feel right now

00:17:12.685 --> 00:17:14.778
isn't reflecting anything happening in the present;

00:17:14.846 --> 00:17:17.566
it's reacting to a threat that hasn't existed for years.

00:17:18.105 --> 00:17:19.538
It's like how the college entrance exams are long gone,

00:17:19.605 --> 00:17:21.338
but so many of us still dream about them,

00:17:21.405 --> 00:17:23.365
and most of the time, it's a nightmare.

00:17:24.046 --> 00:17:25.865
I often talk with my friends about how

00:17:26.046 --> 00:17:29.058
so many people have been stuck in survival mode for so long.

00:17:29.125 --> 00:17:31.499
Always the reactor, always on edge.

00:17:31.566 --> 00:17:33.058
But you're not in that environment anymore.

00:17:33.125 --> 00:17:34.479
You are safe now.

00:17:34.546 --> 00:17:38.365
Yet your system is still running on that old code.

00:17:38.965 --> 00:17:41.419
We don't know how to play in exploration mode.

00:17:41.486 --> 00:17:43.179
We don't know how to go back to being an actor,

00:17:43.246 --> 00:17:46.365
because we've been tense for so long we think it's the only way to live.

00:17:47.105 --> 00:17:49.659
And that scar I mentioned at the start of this video?

00:17:49.726 --> 00:17:51.038
That's my straw man.

00:17:51.105 --> 00:17:54.759
I used lies to bury it in the deepest corner. And by avoiding it,

00:17:54.826 --> 00:17:56.266
I couldn't ever say goodbye to it.

00:17:56.486 --> 00:17:59.459
It pops up from time to time to stop you from being your true

00:17:59.526 --> 00:17:59.979
self.

00:18:00.046 --> 00:18:01.759
So I'm putting it out there today,

00:18:01.826 --> 00:18:03.306
because I finally want to say goodbye to it.

00:18:04.226 --> 00:18:05.858
Your straw man might look different from mine.

00:18:05.925 --> 00:18:08.519
It might be that one time you were rejected as a kid,

00:18:08.586 --> 00:18:10.479
or a moment of public humiliation,

00:18:10.546 --> 00:18:12.719
or the fear you internalized after being punished,

00:18:12.786 --> 00:18:15.006
or the constant hint that you just weren't good enough.

00:18:15.405 --> 00:18:18.318
But whatever it is, it belongs to the past.

00:18:18.385 --> 00:18:19.818
It has no place in your present.

00:18:19.885 --> 00:18:22.519
In The Courage to Be Disliked, which we read before, there's a

00:18:22.586 --> 00:18:23.919
concept a lot of people struggle to accept:

00:18:23.986 --> 00:18:26.105
that trauma doesn't exist.

00:18:26.945 --> 00:18:28.098
It doesn't mean pain isn't real.

00:18:28.165 --> 00:18:32.159
It means your current reaction isn't being forced on you

00:18:32.226 --> 00:18:33.239
by that past event.

00:18:33.306 --> 00:18:34.625
You have a choice.

00:18:35.165 --> 00:18:37.105
This is exactly what Psycho-Cybernetics is saying too.

00:18:37.566 --> 00:18:38.538
The book makes it very clear:

00:18:38.605 --> 00:18:40.098
when faced with a fake problem,

00:18:40.165 --> 00:18:42.226
the best reaction is actually no reaction.

00:18:42.465 --> 00:18:45.659
Reacting makes you a reactor—it makes you tense.

00:18:45.726 --> 00:18:48.365
Not reacting makes you the actor—it makes you relaxed.

00:18:50.405 --> 00:18:52.538
So when we talk about learning to relax,

00:18:52.605 --> 00:18:55.479
it essentially means learning not to react to non-existent problems,

00:18:55.546 --> 00:18:57.199
to problems we've blown completely out of proportion,

00:18:57.266 --> 00:19:00.499
to emotions and environments that can hurt us. Not

00:19:00.566 --> 00:19:01.159
reacting.

00:19:01.226 --> 00:19:03.878
Capturing that relaxation, letting the straw man fall,

00:19:03.945 --> 00:19:07.358
letting your success mechanism genuinely run for you again,

00:19:07.425 --> 00:19:09.219
achieving maximum self-expression,

00:19:09.286 --> 00:19:10.705
and unlocking the next chapter of your life.

00:19:11.326 --> 00:19:14.239
This is why, at 40, I decided to stop pushing so

00:19:14.306 --> 00:19:14.878
hard,

00:19:14.945 --> 00:19:17.405
and made learning to relax a major life priority.

00:19:18.086 --> 00:19:20.885
Naval says there are three things in life: learn, earn, relax.

00:19:21.205 --> 00:19:22.979
I've spent the last 30 years doing the first two,

00:19:23.046 --> 00:19:25.058
and now it's time to start the third.

00:19:25.125 --> 00:19:27.038
Because "Good things only happen when you

00:19:27.105 --> 00:19:32.346
are relaxed."

